{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>When you draw near to a city to fight against it, and you call out to it for peace.</b> The verse speaks of an optional war (מלחמת הרשות), in which one opens with an offer of peace; but in a commanded war (מלחמת מצוה) against the seven nations one does not open with peace — thus Rashi z\"l explained. Yet the verse speaks in general terms: 'when you draw near to a city' — and surely every city and every war is implied, and whether in an optional war or a commanded war one opens with peace, except for Ammon and Moab, concerning whom the verse explicitly stated (Deuteronomy 23:7): 'You shall not seek their peace or their welfare.' And even Ammon and Moab, with whom one does not open with peace — if they made peace of their own accord, one accepts them, to teach you how great is the power of peace." "<b>And now,</b> this passage is a warning to Israel not to wage war against any nation in the world until they call out to it for peace — whether an optional war against the other nations or a commanded war against the seven nations. If they made peace and accepted upon themselves the seven commandments of the sons of Noah and the king's tribute, one does not kill a soul among them, as it says: 'they shall be tribute to you and serve you.' The meaning of the tribute is that they be ready for the king's service with their bodies and their property, such as building walls and fortifying strongholds and building the king's palace and similar things, as it says (I Kings 9:15): 'and this is the matter of the tribute which king Solomon raised to build the house of HaShem and his house and the Milo and the wall of Jerusalem,' etc., 'and all the store-cities which Solomon had.' And if they did not make peace, or even if they made peace but did not accept the seven commandments — if it is an optional war, one kills the adult males, as it says: 'and if it does not make peace with you,' etc., 'and you shall strike every male among it by the sword' — but not the children and the women, as it says: 'only the women and the children' — this refers to male children. But in a commanded war, such as the seven nations and Amalek, one kills all, and this is what it says: 'only from the cities of these peoples which HaShem your Elohim gives you as an inheritance' — these are the seven nations — 'you shall not leave alive any soul' — you are commanded to kill all, even the women and children. And the reason for this: in an optional war we come by virtue of our merit to uproot the power of their constellation and humble it, not to uproot the appointed prince over them, but rather our merit will humble and bring down the power of the constellation so that they do not raise their head against us, but are subjugated to us; therefore it suffices that we kill the males, not the women and children. But in a commanded war, which the Holy One, blessed be He, commanded us, the intent is to uproot the appointed princes over them — for the Holy One, blessed be He, nullifies their power above — therefore we were commanded to uproot all, the women and children, in order to uproot all the power that their princes have below. Therefore the verse distinguished between an optional war and a commanded war, because an optional war is against the constellations, not against the heavenly princes, while a commanded war is against the princes — and therefore we must uproot everything and leave them nothing, for through this they will be uprooted and nullified from the world, both above and below." "<b>And if</b> your heart hesitates to say that we commit violence against the children who have not sinned against us — behold, this is a heavenly decree and the pronouncement of the verse. Moreover, since the Holy One, blessed be He, uproots their power above, whatever we do to them below is as though we did nothing at all, as the Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: 'flour — it is ground flour; the lion — it is a slain lion; the city — it is a burned city' — and there is no violence in this, and it is not counted as killing, for they are already slain. Furthermore, even if they were not counted as slain, there is no violence in killing children, for they are branches of the root of rebellion — the bitter and reckless nation — for they will surely follow the ways of their fathers to do every abomination of HaShem which He hates, and they will teach Israel from them; and so the verse mentioned in this passage: 'so that they do not teach you to do according to all their abominations.' And perhaps you will say: when they grow up they may enter the covenant and repent — go out and learn from the One who permits their blood, the Holy One, blessed be He, who knows that they will not repent. And thus Isaiah of blessed memory said explicitly (Isaiah 14:21): 'Prepare a slaughter for his children because of the iniquity of their fathers; they shall not rise nor inherit the earth nor fill the face of the world with cities.' If so, if we were to leave them alive, their lives would be a cause for great harm, greater than the harm of killing them; and it is reasonable before reason that a person should do a small harm in order to prevent a great harm. For a thoughtful and wise man will throw himself from the roof to escape danger, or will cut off his hand or his foot or one of his limbs to save his whole body, or will drink a bitter draught such as wormwood in order to drive away illness from himself — and in all this he is not doing violence to himself, but rather doing kindness with himself to keep his soul alive. And since a person does so for himself and cannot be judged as committing violence — all the more so it is not violence when one does so for the sake of others. And for this reason the Torah permitted the killing of children, to do a small harm in order to prevent a great harm that would have come to the world through their lives, and there is no violence in this; rather, it is a matter of reason for one who reflects upon it." "<b>And now,</b> when the verse permitted killing all the women and children, that is only when they did not make peace; but if they made peace — all the nations are equal regarding peace, even the seven nations. For thus it is written in Joshua (11): 'There was not a city that made peace with the children of Israel except the Hivite, inhabitants of Gibeon; all the rest they took in battle, for it was from HaShem to strengthen their hearts to come against the war against Israel in order to destroy them.' This implies that he sent them word of peace and they did not accept. And thus we learned in the Sifrei: three letters did Joshua send before he entered the land — the first he sent them: whoever wishes to make peace, let him come and make peace; he again sent: whoever wishes to evacuate, let him evacuate; he again sent: whoever wishes to make war, let him make war. And they said in the Jerusalem Talmud: the Gibeonites made peace; the Girgashite evacuated; thirty-one kings made war."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {2 "The Aramaic phrase 'קמחא טחינא טחינת...' is a Talmudic idiom (cf. Sanhedrin 82a and parallels) indicating a deed already accomplished; rendered here hyper-literally to preserve the idiom."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:50:00.213987845Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}